WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.670
When a question asks for the largest possible number,

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.790
trying examples is not enough.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.730
You need a reason why nobody could ever do better.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:12.040
That reason usually comes from something limited that

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:13.670
every arrangement has to use.

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:16.950
Finding a good arrangement shows what is possible.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.350
It does not show that it is the best.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:22.560
Without a proof of the limit, there is always the worry

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.530
that a cleverer arrangement exists.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:28.290
Counters are placed in a row of eight cells,

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:29.950
at most one per cell.

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:32.790
No two counters may be in neighbouring cells.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.540
What is the largest number of counters that

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:36.190
can be placed?

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:39.670
Pause and try to find both an arrangement and a reason.

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:46.380
Split the row into four pairs of neighbouring cells.

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:48.950
Each pair can hold at most one counter,

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:51.800
because two counters in a pair would be neighbours.

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:55.060
Four pairs means at most four counters.

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:56.440
That is the limit.

00:00:57.370 --> 00:00:58.260
Now reach the limit.

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:01.240
Put counters in cells one, three, five and seven.

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:02.520
No two are neighbours.

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:05.740
So four is possible, and more than four is impossible.

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:08.000
The largest number is four.

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:11.770
With nine cells, there are four pairs and one

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:12.920
cell left over.

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:16.610
Each pair holds at most one counter and the extra cell

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:18.960
holds at most one: at most five.

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:23.360
And cells one, three, five, seven and nine achieve it.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:27.460
For a smallest-number question, turn the idea around:

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:30.120
ask what every arrangement must cover.

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:33.280
If every empty cell must be next to a counter,

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:36.200
each counter can look after at most three cells,

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:38.080
itself and its two neighbours.

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:41.050
Eight cells need at least three counters.

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:44.560
Cells two, five and eight give that coverage,

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:46.160
so three is enough.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:48.150
Find what is limited.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:49.830
Use it to prove a limit.

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:52.350
Then show an arrangement that reaches the limit.

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:55.580
Notebook challenge: what is the largest number of

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:57.660
counters in a row of eleven cells with

00:01:57.660 --> 00:01:58.410
no two neighbours?
